<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="Authentication-OAuth2" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>
        Many email providers, including Gmail and Microsoft 365 (Exchange Online), have disabled (or are in the
        process of disabling) password-based authentication for IMAP, POP3 and SMTP. Instead, applications must
        obtain an OAuth 2.0 access token from the provider and use it to authenticate.
      </para>
      <para>
        MailKit does not obtain access tokens itself. Use the provider's authentication library (such as the
        Microsoft Authentication Library (MSAL) or the Google APIs client library) to obtain a token, then pass
        it to MailKit using one of the OAuth SASL mechanisms described below.
      </para>
      <autoOutline />
    </introduction>

    <section address="Mechanisms">
      <title>XOAUTH2 and OAUTHBEARER</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          There are two SASL mechanisms for authenticating with an OAuth 2.0 access token:
        </para>
        <table>
          <tableHeader>
            <row>
              <entry><para>Mechanism</para></entry>
              <entry><para>Class</para></entry>
              <entry><para>Description</para></entry>
            </row>
          </tableHeader>
          <row>
            <entry><para>OAUTHBEARER</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismOAuthBearer</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                The standard mechanism, defined in
                <externalLink>
                  <linkText>rfc7628</linkText>
                  <linkUri>https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc7628</linkUri>
                  <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
                </externalLink>.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>XOAUTH2</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismOAuth2</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                A proprietary mechanism created by Google that predates <literal>OAUTHBEARER</literal>. It is still
                the most widely supported, including by Gmail and Microsoft 365.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </table>
        <para>
          Both take the user name (usually the email address) and the access token. Since neither mechanism is
          chosen automatically, check <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailService.AuthenticationMechanisms</codeEntityReference>
          and prefer <literal>OAUTHBEARER</literal> when the server advertises it:
        </para>
        <code language="c#">
SaslMechanism oauth2;

if (client.AuthenticationMechanisms.Contains ("OAUTHBEARER"))
    oauth2 = new SaslMechanismOAuthBearer (username, accessToken);
else
    oauth2 = new SaslMechanismOAuth2 (username, accessToken);

client.Authenticate (oauth2);
        </code>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="MicrosoftExchange">
      <title>Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          For Exchange Online, register your application in Microsoft Entra ID, grant it the
          <literal>IMAP.AccessAsUser.All</literal>, <literal>POP.AccessAsUser.All</literal> and/or
          <literal>SMTP.Send</literal> permissions and use MSAL to acquire a token. The following example uses a
          <literal>PublicClientApplication</literal> for a desktop application:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\OAuth2ExchangeExample.cs"/>
        <para>
          Web applications and background services (daemons) require a different flow, and services also require
          additional configuration in Exchange Online. See
          <externalLink>
            <linkText>Using OAuth2 With Exchange (IMAP, POP3 or SMTP)</linkText>
            <linkUri>https://github.com/jstedfast/MailKit/blob/master/ExchangeOAuth2.md</linkUri>
            <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
          </externalLink>
          for complete step-by-step instructions.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Gmail">
      <title>Gmail</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          For Gmail, create an OAuth client ID in the Google Cloud Console, request the
          <literal>https://mail.google.com/</literal> scope and use the Google APIs client library to acquire a
          token:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\OAuth2GMailExample.cs"/>
        <para>
          See
          <externalLink>
            <linkText>Using OAuth2 With GMail (IMAP, POP3 or SMTP)</linkText>
            <linkUri>https://github.com/jstedfast/MailKit/blob/master/GMailOAuth2.md</linkUri>
            <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
          </externalLink>
          for complete step-by-step instructions, including how to authenticate from an ASP.NET web application.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="TokenLifetime">
      <title>Token lifetime</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Access tokens are short-lived (typically about an hour). Rather than storing an access token, store the
          refresh token (or let the authentication library cache it for you) and request a fresh access token
          each time you connect. Both of the examples above do this, using <literal>AcquireTokenSilent</literal>
          and <literal>RefreshTokenAsync</literal> respectively.
        </para>
        <para>
          If the token has expired or does not have the required scopes, authentication fails with an
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.AuthenticationException</codeEntityReference>. The most common
          causes are requesting the wrong scope (for example, a Microsoft Graph scope instead of an
          <literal>outlook.office.com</literal> scope), using the wrong user name, or IMAP/POP3/SMTP AUTH being
          disabled for the mailbox by an administrator.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <relatedTopics>
      <link xlink:href="Authentication"/>
      <link xlink:href="Authentication-NTLM-Kerberos"/>
    </relatedTopics>
  </developerConceptualDocument>
</topic>
